## Separation logic: the basics

{pause}
### Assertions

{pause}
- In mathematical logic, we manipulate *propositions*.

{pause}
- In separation logic, we manipulate **assertions**.

{pause}
- An assertion says something about an implicit **heap fragment**.

{pause up}
### "Points-to" assertions

```math
p \mapsto v
```

{pause}
"$p$ points to $v$"

{pause}
"The variable $p$ holds the address of a memory location that contains the value $v$"

<style>
  .slipshow-svg-container { display: block; }
  .slipshow-svg-container svg {
    display: block;
    margin-left: auto;
    margin-right: auto;
    width: auto;
    zoom: 1.4;
  }
</style>

{pause}
![Diagram 1](heap1.dot.svg)

{pause}
### Footprints

{.definition pause}
The **footprint** of an assertion is the set the locations that are mentioned in the assertion.

{pause up}
### Separating conjunction

{pause}
- In mathematical logic, given two propositions $A$ and $B$, we can form their conjunction:

{pause}
```math
A \land B
```

{pause}
- In separation logic, given two assertions $A$ and $B$, we can form their **separating conjunction**:

{pause}
```math
A * B
```

{pause}
Pronounced "$A$ and separately $B$".

{pause}
Approximately:

{pause}
```math
A \land B \land \text{"the memory footprints of $A$ and $B$ are disjoint"}
```

{pause center}
> #### Example:
> 
> $p \mapsto \text{"hello"} * q \mapsto \text{"goodbye"}$:
> 
> ![Diagram 2](heap2.dot.svg)

{pause center}
> #### Another example (always false):
>
> ```math
> p \mapsto \text{"hello"} * p \mapsto \text{"hello"}
> ```

{pause up}
